1. Core architecture you will implement now
Frontend: Next.js + React + TypeScript
Styling: Tailwind CSS + shadcn/ui
Charts: Recharts
Auth, DB, Storage: Supabase free tier
AI:
Client side Whisper tiny (preview after recording)
Client side Whisper small (final scoring before saving to DB)
Simple grammar checker (rule based, local)
SpeechSynthesis API for reference pronunciation
Post recording feedback only for now. No streaming.
2. Database schema in logical form
Supabase already has auth.users. You add your own tables in public schema.
I will define:

profiles
study_groups
group_members
assignments
assignment_questions
sessions
results
result_errors
comments
reference_words
You can later convert this to SQL.

2.1 profiles
Link Supabase user to role and extra info.

id: uuid, primary key, references auth.users.id
full_name: text
role: text check in ('student', 'teacher', 'admin')
created_at: timestamptz default now()
updated_at: timestamptz
2.2 study_groups
id: uuid primary key
name: text not null  (example "4A group 16:30")
description: text
created_by: uuid references profiles(id)
created_at: timestamptz default now()
2.3 group_members
id: uuid primary key
group_id: uuid references study_groups(id)
user_id: uuid references profiles(id)
role_in_group: text default 'student'
joined_at: timestamptz default now()
One teacher can be a member with role_in_group 'teacher'.

2.4 assignments
Reading or essay tasks.

id: uuid primary key
group_id: uuid references study_groups(id)
created_by: uuid references profiles(id)
type: text check in ('reading', 'essay')
title: text
text_content: text
min_words: integer default 0  (for essay)
is_active: boolean default true
created_at: timestamptz
updated_at: timestamptz
2.5 assignment_questions
For Exercise 1 questions.

id: uuid primary key
assignment_id: uuid references assignments(id)
question_text: text
expected_answer_text: text  (for exact or near exact match)
order_index: integer
2.6 sessions
Each attempt of a student on an assignment.

id: uuid primary key
user_id: uuid references profiles(id)
assignment_id: uuid references assignments(id)
started_at: timestamptz
finished_at: timestamptz
attempt_number: integer  (1, 2, 3, etc per student per assignment)
audio_url: text  (Supabase Storage, last attempt overwrites file, but each session row still exists as history)
You decided to keep multiple attempts in DB but only store one audio file. So the audio_url for older sessions may point to the same latest file. That is acceptable.

2.7 results
Aggregated numeric result per session.

id: uuid primary key
session_id: uuid references sessions(id)
pronunciation_score: numeric  (0 to 100)
grammar_score: numeric
coherence_score: numeric
overall_score: numeric
words_total: integer
words_correct: integer
words_incorrect: integer
questions_total: integer
questions_correct: integer
created_at: timestamptz
2.8 result_errors
Detailed per word or per phrase error list.

id: uuid primary key
result_id: uuid references results(id)
word_index: integer  (position in reference text)
reference_word: text
recognized_word: text
error_type: text check in ('missing', 'extra', 'substitution', 'pronunciation', 'grammar')
comment: text
This table allows teacher analytics: most frequent error words, patterns.

2.9 comments
Teacher feedback per session or result.

id: uuid primary key
result_id: uuid references results(id)
teacher_id: uuid references profiles(id)
text: text
created_at: timestamptz
is_seen_by_student: boolean default false
2.10 reference_words
Reference pronunciation data per word.

id: uuid primary key
word: text
language: text default 'en'
audio_url: text null  (if you ever store generated TTS)
usage_count: integer default 0
created_by_teacher_id: uuid references profiles(id) null
For now you can generate pronunciation with SpeechSynthesis on demand without storing anything, and only later use this table.
3. Auth, roles, signup
You chose separate signup pages plus self registration and Google OAuth.

3.1 Signup flows
/signup-student
User chooses email password or Google.
After Supabase signup, create profile row with role 'student'.
/signup-teacher
Same, but profile role 'teacher'.
Later you can add an optional secret field to avoid random people signing up as teachers.

3.2 Login flow
/login
simple credential or OAuth login
front end calls Supabase Auth
fetch profile and redirect:
role student → student dashboard
role teacher → teacher dashboard
4. API outline for Next.js
Most logic is client side plus Supabase direct calls, so API routes will be small.
I suggest these API groups:

4.1 /api/student/assignments
GET list of assignments visible to current student in his groups.
4.2 /api/student/sessions
POST create session record when student starts an attempt.
PATCH finish session (set finished_at and attempt_number).
4.3 /api/student/results
POST to save final result and error list after Whisper small analysis on the client.

Payload:
session_id
numeric scores
word stats
detailed errors array
4.4 /api/teacher/assignments
GET assignments created by teacher.
POST create new assignment with text and questions.
PATCH update assignment.
4.5 /api/teacher/analytics
Endpoints to get:

per student progress
per group distribution
top error words
For example:

GET /api/teacher/analytics/group/:groupId
GET /api/teacher/analytics/student/:studentId
These routes mainly do SQL aggregations on results and result_errors.
5. Main flows for Exercise 1 and 2
5.1 Exercise 1: reading plus questions
Front end steps:

Student chooses assignment of type 'reading'.
Page loads reference text and questions from Supabase.
Student presses "Start recording".
MediaRecorder records audio until "Stop".
Front end:
runs Whisper tiny on recorded blob
gets transcript
compares with reference using Levenshtein diff
shows preview errors on screen
Student presses "Submit attempt".
Front end:
runs Whisper small for final transcript
recomputes diff and scores
computes pronunciation_score, words stats
sends result and errors to /api/student/results
uploads audio blob to Supabase Storage at a path that overwrites previous file for that assignment and user
updates session row with audio_url
Student answers comprehension questions by voice one by one:
each answer is transcribed with tiny or small
compared to expected_answer_text using simple text similarity
Final page shows:
text with colored words
score per criterion
list of incorrect words clickable
correct answers and wrong answers
5.2 Exercise 2: mini essay dictation
Student chooses assignment of type 'essay'.
Page shows topic.
Student dictates essay audio.
Whisper tiny or small transcribes essay.
Simple grammar and word count module checks:
total words
sentence count
basic errors like repeated spaces, no capital letter at start, no final punctuation, very short sentences.
If word count < min_words:
show message "minimum word count not reached, please add more text"
do not save result record.
If ok:
compute:
grammar_score: based on number of simple rule violations
coherence_score: simple heuristics (presence of topic keywords, connectors like "because", "however").
save result row.
All NLP remains local rule based.
6. Implementation roadmap for semester 1
You want AI vibe coding. That means you ask the model per small task, for example "generate Next.js page for /student/tasks" and then you incrementally implement.
I suggest this order.

Stage 1: project skeleton
Create Next.js app with TypeScript.
Add Tailwind and shadcn/ui.
Configure Supabase client in frontend.
Configure Supabase tables using the schema above.
Stage 2: auth and profiles
Implement /signup-student, /signup-teacher, /login pages.
On first login, ensure profile row exists with correct role.
Role based redirects:
Student dashboard
Teacher dashboard
Stage 3: basic student flows without AI
Student dashboard: list assignments from Supabase.
Assignment page: show text and questions, no speech yet.
Mark assignment as "completed" manually to test progress display.
Stage 4: audio recording and Whisper tiny
Add Web Audio API recording on assignment page.
Integrate @xenova/transformers Whisper tiny client side.
Run transcription after recording and print transcript to console.
Implement local Levenshtein diff to compare transcript with reference text.
Render text with colored words.
Stage 5: final scoring with Whisper small
Lazy load Whisper small only when user clicks Submit.
Recompute transcript and scores.
Compute pronunciation_score and words stats.
Send results to Supabase via API or direct insert.
Stage 6: audio upload and sessions
Create session row when recording starts.
Upload audio to Supabase Storage when submitting.
Update session row with audio_url and associate result with the session.
Stage 7: teacher dashboard
Teacher dashboard page shows groups and students (simple first).
Add assignment creation form.
Add analytics charts using Recharts, using results and result_errors tables.
Stage 8: simple essay mode
Create essay assignment type with topic text.
Implement dictation, transcription, simple grammar checking and word count.
Display basic feedback and scores.
At each step, you can ask the AI to generate concrete code for a single task, for example:

"Generate a Next.js page at /student/assignments that lists assignments from Supabase."
"Generate a React hook that handles MediaRecorder for microphone input and returns a Blob."